WEBVTT

00:00.677 --> 00:04.700
Diagnosing Compositional Generalization
in Sequential Robot Tasks.

00:05.200 --> 00:07.222
Imagine a robot that picks an object,

00:07.222 --> 00:09.844
places it in a container,
then presses a button.

00:10.084 --> 00:12.999
With four options each, that's
already sixty-four tasks.

00:13.239 --> 00:17.918
Each extra subtask multiplies that, so
demonstrating everything is impractical.

00:18.158 --> 00:20.798
Real datasets are often
sparse: every object,

00:20.798 --> 00:23.848
container, and button appears,
but few combinations.

00:24.088 --> 00:27.670
And policies often fail on new
combinations of familiar parts.

00:27.910 --> 00:31.311
So which combinations does a
robot actually need to practice?

00:31.811 --> 00:34.955
We bound the generalization
gap by three sources of shift.

00:35.195 --> 00:37.991
Marginal shift: is each
instruction value covered?

00:38.231 --> 00:43.283
Instruction-compositional shift: are familiar
values recombined in unfamiliar ways?

00:43.523 --> 00:46.454
And context-action shift:
do those combinations

00:46.454 --> 00:48.887
induce unfamiliar contexts and actions?

00:49.127 --> 00:50.573
That raises three questions.

00:51.073 --> 00:52.959
First, is full coverage necessary?

00:53.199 --> 00:55.950
We fix the demonstration
budget, train on B tasks

00:55.950 --> 00:58.363
covering every value,
and test on the rest.

00:58.603 --> 01:02.360
With four tasks, held-out
success is only 0.4 percent.

01:02.600 --> 01:07.320
It climbs with more tasks, then saturates
around sixteen, far below sixty-four.

01:07.560 --> 01:11.877
Why? Held-out tasks sharing more instruction
pairs with training succeed more often.

01:12.117 --> 01:16.724
So we test an orthogonal design: every
pair of values appears exactly once.

01:16.964 --> 01:21.771
With one quarter of the tasks, it
reaches 78.2 percent held-out,

01:21.771 --> 01:24.025
close to full training's 78.6.

01:24.525 --> 01:27.100
Second, when sparse coverage
fails, what is missing?

01:27.340 --> 01:30.250
Train on four tasks: 83 percent on those,

01:30.250 --> 01:32.806
only 0.4 percent on the other sixty.

01:33.046 --> 01:36.010
Yet those tasks show every
skill: grasping each object,

01:36.010 --> 01:38.596
placing in each container,
pressing each button.

01:38.836 --> 01:41.527
So is it missing skills,
or unable to steer them?

01:41.767 --> 01:46.765
Finetuning with one demonstration
per task lifts them to 54.7 percent.

01:47.005 --> 01:50.860
The same demonstrations from
scratch reach only 0.3 percent.

01:51.100 --> 01:53.085
This suggests the skills are reusable.

01:53.085 --> 01:55.123
What's missing is instruction steering.

01:55.623 --> 01:57.930
Third, what if instructions
depend on each other?

01:58.170 --> 02:01.235
Here the robot places a cube,
then puts milk on the plate.

02:01.475 --> 02:04.589
The cube's container isn't given,
but it can't be the plate,

02:04.589 --> 02:07.392
so the first placement depends
on a later instruction.

02:07.632 --> 02:12.208
With sixteen tasks, we vary coverage of
first-object, second-container pairs.

02:12.448 --> 02:17.473
From six pairs to sixteen, held-out
success rises from 9 to 31 percent,

02:17.473 --> 02:20.729
and wrong-container errors
fall from 21 to 14.

02:20.969 --> 02:23.489
A modular policy adds
an oracle that passes

02:23.489 --> 02:25.951
only the active instruction at each stage.

02:26.191 --> 02:28.585
On independent tasks, that's enough:

02:28.585 --> 02:30.248
72 percent at four tasks.

02:30.488 --> 02:33.119
But while placing the cube,
it sees only the cube.

02:33.119 --> 02:35.488
The plate is hidden, so
it can't rule it out.

02:35.728 --> 02:38.416
Wrong-container errors
stay near 24 percent,

02:38.416 --> 02:41.227
even with full coverage:
an information limit.

02:41.727 --> 02:43.953
So: full enumeration is unnecessary,

02:43.953 --> 02:46.675
sparse failures can be
steering, not skills,

02:46.675 --> 02:49.459
and dependent tasks need
relational coverage.

02:49.699 --> 02:53.818
Collect data to cover the dependencies
that matter, not every combination.

02:54.058 --> 02:58.751
Paper, code, data, and interactive results
are on our project website. Thank you.
